WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.435 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I don't know exactly
how the woke people

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.037
got in charge here
in so many places.

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:05.271 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's partially 'cause
they're so loud.

00:00:05.271 --> 00:00:06.673
I think there's 5 or
10% of people here

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.241 align:start size:81% position:19%
who really believe
in that stuff.

00:00:08.241 --> 00:00:09.476
And they've gotten very good.

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:10.677 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think it came from
the universities.

00:00:10.677 --> 00:00:12.112 align:start size:84% position:16%
You look at our
big tech companies.

00:00:12.112 --> 00:00:14.247 align:start size:97% position:3%
The culture at Google is
really modeled off of Stanford

00:00:14.247 --> 00:00:15.448
'cause of all the PhDs,

00:00:15.448 --> 00:00:16.950
same thing with a lot
of the other ones.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:19.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so these tech companies
are basically taking a culture

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:23.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
from a university that's
been broken for a few decades

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.959 align:start position:0%
and these woke people are
really good at punishing people

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:27.861
who stand up to them
and trying to get them.

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:29.262
And so it's just not worth
people's time to push back.

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:31.297
And so, if you're just
trying to do your job,

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:35.568
you're just doing what you can.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:38.405
Seeing these people get to be
in charge, it's really bad.

00:00:38.405 --> 00:00:40.874
(light music)

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:50.984 line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin,

00:00:50.984 --> 00:00:52.685 line:0%
and this is "The Rubin
Report" Locals Week.

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:55.221 line:0%
Joining me today is an
entrepreneur and investor

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:58.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
and policy maker at the Cicero
Institute, Joe Lonsdale,

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.427
welcome to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:01.928
- Thanks, Dave. Good to be on.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:04.931
- Joe I've wanted to have
you on for quite some time,

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:07.600
because you're involved
in about a billion things

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:11.237
that ancillarily are
all about the things

00:01:11.237 --> 00:01:15.308 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I talk about on this
show, from tech to politics,

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:18.378 align:start size:84% position:16%
free speech,
education, all of it.

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:20.647
But first let's start
with those two words

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:23.416
that I mentioned up front,
entrepreneur and investor.

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:25.618
I feel like when you
say that to people,

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:28.054 align:start size:88% position:13%
people think, oh, you
were just born that way.

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:31.691
How does somebody become an
entrepreneur and investor?

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:33.059
I suspect a lot of my audience

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:34.761 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
would like to have
those two tags.

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.898 line:0%
- There's a lot of different
types of this, but for me,

00:01:38.898 --> 00:01:41.534 line:0%
it's about, you see things
and they work a certain way

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:44.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you're frustrated
and you want to fix them.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:46.206
I think having a vision of
how the world should work,

00:01:46.206 --> 00:01:48.475
that's not how it's
working right now,

00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:50.076 align:start size:84% position:16%
that'll lead one to
being an entrepreneur.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.678
'Cause that's basically
the only way to do things

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:53.012
to evolve the economy.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:54.747
- So how'd you get started?

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:56.683 align:start size:88% position:13%
For the guy out there
watching this right now,

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:59.686 align:start size:97% position:3%
going, okay, I got a regular
job, but I've got an idea

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.621
or I'm passionate
about something,

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:03.723
how did it work for you?

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:06.259 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, you know, technology
is a really big part

00:02:06.259 --> 00:02:07.827 align:start size:97% position:3%
of what you need to be a
great entrepreneur these days.

00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:09.129
You don't have to
be a technologist,

00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:11.331 align:start size:88% position:13%
but it's a big advantage
to learn technology,

00:02:11.331 --> 00:02:12.565
learn computer science.

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:15.235
I grew up in the Bay
Area. I was very lucky.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:16.769
I was born in Silicon Valley,

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.872
had a bunch of friends who
were great technologists.

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:20.473
They taught me from a young age.

00:02:20.473 --> 00:02:22.142
I went to Stanford,
computer science

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:23.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
and met a lot of
great technologists.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:25.345
When I was there. I saw a
lot of the smartest people

00:02:25.345 --> 00:02:26.813
were going to work at PayPal.

00:02:28.248 --> 00:02:30.183
At the time it was 2000,
what's this PayPal thing?

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.721 align:start size:88% position:13%
There's one company
started by Peter Thiel,

00:02:34.721 --> 00:02:36.556 align:start size:84% position:16%
and one company
started by Elon Musk

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.057
and they merged and
they made PayPal.

00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:39.759 align:start size:97% position:3%
So of course, Peter brought
his smartest friends.

00:02:39.759 --> 00:02:41.394
Elon brought his
smartest friends.

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:42.795
And I said, wow, I
want to work there.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they actually rejected
me the first time,

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.166
but I tried again
and they let me in,

00:02:46.166 --> 00:02:47.700
and I learned a lot from them.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.802 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I think what you want is
follow the best technologists,

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:52.105
learn technology and
go study other people

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:53.406
who know what they're doing.

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:54.841
And that's probably the
good way to get started.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:58.645 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So as a guy that was
involved in those magic years

00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:02.015
of Silicon Valley and of
big tech and all that,

00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:04.918
when you look back on it, does
it feel like a dream sort of,

00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:07.520 align:start size:81% position:19%
seeing the way
big tech has moved

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
and how the
landscape has changed

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:12.091 align:start size:88% position:13%
and how people generally
talk about big tech

00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:15.028
versus probably what you were
seeing when it was being born?

00:03:15.028 --> 00:03:16.563
Does it all just
feel very weird?

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:19.132
'Cause it's not that
long ago, as you said.

00:03:19.132 --> 00:03:21.334
- Yeah. I remember being
at a dinner with Peter.

00:03:21.334 --> 00:03:24.270
He was the first investor in
Facebook and Mark was there.

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.538
I know the Facebook
founders really well,

00:03:25.538 --> 00:03:26.906
of course from this background,

00:03:26.906 --> 00:03:28.341
I remember being jealous

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:29.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the other really
important people at the table

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:31.511
were treating Mark as
more important than me.

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:34.614
And I said Palantir's going
to be a really big company.

00:03:34.614 --> 00:03:36.282 align:start position:0%
Why do you think Facebook's
necessarily going to be bigger?

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:38.618
So obviously I was wrong

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:40.420 align:start size:94% position:6%
that we were gonna be
nearly as big as Facebook.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.722
I went public last
week for $22 billion.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:43.990
So we didn't do horribly.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:47.594
We're not worth five,

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:49.462 align:start size:84% position:16%
whatever Mark's worth
trillion dollars.

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:52.865 align:start size:97% position:3%
It is interesting having known
a lot of these characters.

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:55.068 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're very ardent,
very hardworking people.

00:03:55.068 --> 00:03:56.603
I don't think any of us knew

00:03:56.603 --> 00:03:58.204
how big these companies
were going to get though.

00:03:58.204 --> 00:04:00.773
- I like how you just,
poo-pooed 22 billion.

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:02.642
Yeah, 22 billion last week.

00:04:02.642 --> 00:04:04.177
We'll get to that, by the way.

00:04:04.177 --> 00:04:05.378 align:start size:88% position:13%
- There's always
someone who's crushed it

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:06.846
and done way better
than you, right?

00:04:06.846 --> 00:04:09.082
At least, that's
been my experience.

00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:10.583
- It's kind of funny.

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:11.517
I was actually going to get
there a little bit later,

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:12.785
but let's just jump to it.

00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:14.988
Do the numbers when you
say something like that,

00:04:14.988 --> 00:04:17.423
so Palantir went
to IPO $22 billion,

00:04:17.423 --> 00:04:20.260
the numbers start to seem crazy.

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:24.230
Do they seem crazy even to
you guys that are in it?

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.865
Because I think to
the average person,

00:04:25.865 --> 00:04:28.301
you can't fathom
what $1 billion is.

00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:32.305
I don't think most people can
fathom what $10 million is.

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:38.111
- I guess in some ways I'm lucky

00:04:38.111 --> 00:04:41.414 align:start size:97% position:3%
that it took so long
because as things take time

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:43.283
over the years, you
kind of adjust to it.

00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:46.352
I think if it were to all of
a sudden fall in your lap,

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:47.620
after a couple of years,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.856
it would probably be
extremely unhealthy.

00:04:49.856 --> 00:04:52.558
There was things I worked
on with Peter, at the fund

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:55.228 align:start size:97% position:3%
where we had a hedge fund
that kind of blew up one year

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:57.196 align:start size:88% position:13%
where I would've
gotten this giant bonus

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:59.866
as a 25 year old and I
didn't and looking back,

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:01.334 align:start size:81% position:19%
it's probably good
that I didn't.

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:03.036
'Cause if you just get a
bunch of money all at once,

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:04.304
it's hard to adjust to it.

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:06.072
If you build these
things over time,

00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:08.741
you kinda learn
how to manage it.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:11.144
You learn the money is not
like a bunch of gold coins.

00:05:11.144 --> 00:05:12.345
If you asked me as a kid,

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:13.813 align:start size:88% position:13%
I would've thought I
was like Scrooge McDuck.

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:16.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
And there's like a tower,
and there's gold coins in it.

00:05:16.716 --> 00:05:18.184
That's what money is,
but it's actually-

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.286
- You don't dive into your
tower with a big dollar sign?

00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:24.023
- Maybe at some point we
should figure that out.

00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:28.094 align:start size:84% position:16%
I just had break
Peter Thiel yesterday,

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:31.164
he made more on Palantir of
course, than PayPal is worth.

00:05:33.633 --> 00:05:35.835
When it was sold to eBay, of
course, when he first did it,

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:39.706
but these people, the money
represents these missions

00:05:39.706 --> 00:05:42.208
and these causes and
the money is ownership

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:43.476
and something you've built.

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:46.512
So it becomes much more
about, the money, sure,

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:48.314 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's worth some large
amount on paper,

00:05:48.314 --> 00:05:49.682
but it's much more
about the ability

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:50.817
to get things done in the world,

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:51.751
ability to influence things.

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:53.019
At some point it's not money

00:05:53.019 --> 00:05:53.953
you're gonna spend for yourself,

00:05:53.953 --> 00:05:55.154
it's money you're gonna use

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:56.622
to achieve stuff
and to build stuff.

00:05:56.622 --> 00:05:58.758
- Do you think that's the
fundamental disconnect

00:05:58.758 --> 00:06:00.193
when we talk about money

00:06:00.193 --> 00:06:02.061 align:start size:94% position:6%
and when there's a certain
level of resentment,

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:03.863
when these companies go to IPO

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:07.400 align:start size:94% position:6%
or when they see people
getting these huge payouts

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.969
or something that they
don't really understand

00:06:09.969 --> 00:06:11.137
the work that it took,

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:12.905 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the way these
things change industries

00:06:12.905 --> 00:06:14.374
and everything else?

00:06:14.374 --> 00:06:16.843 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's a disconnect between
what's actually going on

00:06:16.843 --> 00:06:18.344
with a lot of these companies

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:22.281
and what the media
portrays it all as.

00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:24.517 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Of course it's a
massive amount of work.

00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:26.085
And of course, a lot of people

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:28.087 align:start position:0%
wouldn't take bets on these
things, wouldn't invest in them,

00:06:28.087 --> 00:06:30.289 align:start size:88% position:13%
wouldn't do them if
there wasn't an economy

00:06:30.289 --> 00:06:31.524 align:start size:84% position:16%
where you could
earn lots of money.

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:33.659 align:start size:81% position:19%
But I think the
biggest disconnect

00:06:33.659 --> 00:06:36.262 align:start size:81% position:19%
is that there's
really two options

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:38.030 align:start size:84% position:16%
with these resources
we're creating,

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.699 align:start size:84% position:16%
is once the resource
has been created

00:06:39.699 --> 00:06:41.467 align:start size:88% position:13%
either you're gonna have
a bunch of committees

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:43.436
and bureaucrats in
charge of that money,

00:06:43.436 --> 00:06:45.838
or you're gonna have these
really hardworking people

00:06:45.838 --> 00:06:47.173
who know how to build
things in the world

00:06:47.173 --> 00:06:49.041
like Elon Musk and Peter Thiel,

00:06:49.041 --> 00:06:51.177 align:start size:97% position:3%
and all sorts of other
people with different views,

00:06:51.177 --> 00:06:54.414
who've created this money,
using it as they see fit.

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:57.283
It's amazing.

00:06:57.283 --> 00:06:58.484
Most of the money in the world
now is running by committees.

00:06:58.484 --> 00:06:59.752
Most of the money is
taken by pension funds,

00:06:59.752 --> 00:07:00.987
sovereign wealth funds.

00:07:00.987 --> 00:07:02.722 align:start size:81% position:19%
Like 98% of the
money in the world

00:07:02.722 --> 00:07:04.724
is controlled by
these big, slow,

00:07:04.724 --> 00:07:06.893
boring things like
insurance companies.

00:07:06.893 --> 00:07:09.228
And those guys don't
usually do anything

00:07:09.228 --> 00:07:10.463
that's that interesting.

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:12.265
Whereas there's this
small number of people

00:07:12.265 --> 00:07:13.332
who've created wealth

00:07:14.934 --> 00:07:16.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
that's like the last
free wealth in the world.

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:17.637
And the people want to take that

00:07:17.637 --> 00:07:18.871 align:start size:81% position:19%
and give it to the
committees too.

00:07:18.871 --> 00:07:20.273
It's like, what
are you thinking?

00:07:20.273 --> 00:07:21.974
Don't you want people
who know how to build

00:07:21.974 --> 00:07:23.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
taking this and using
it for something?

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:25.745
- So you're giving me
the perfect segue to,

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:28.347
I think something that a
lot of people wonder about,

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:30.817 align:start size:84% position:16%
how is it that a place
like Silicon Valley

00:07:30.817 --> 00:07:33.519 align:start size:97% position:3%
that has so much of the spirit
that you're talking about,

00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:35.254
these people who want
to change the world,

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:38.224 align:start size:97% position:3%
who want to invent and
innovate and the rest of it,

00:07:38.224 --> 00:07:42.295
how is it that the
social justice thing,

00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:44.797
which is so counter
to that in many ways,

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:47.633
how did it infect almost
everything to the point

00:07:47.633 --> 00:07:50.102
that everybody's leaving
San Francisco right now?

00:07:51.204 --> 00:07:53.906
- Yeah, it's a huge problem

00:07:53.906 --> 00:07:55.541
in the culture of
Silicon Valley.

00:07:57.543 --> 00:08:00.213
This has been the case,
for a very long time

00:08:00.213 --> 00:08:01.981
is when you're very successful

00:08:01.981 --> 00:08:03.749
and wealthy and philanthropic,

00:08:03.749 --> 00:08:07.787 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think a lot of people will
have some level of guilt

00:08:07.787 --> 00:08:09.188
about their success.

00:08:09.188 --> 00:08:11.824 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then the guilt, doesn't
have to be rational.

00:08:11.824 --> 00:08:13.025
It's just like, you're guilty.

00:08:13.025 --> 00:08:13.960
There's people who
are poor in the world

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:15.728
and you have success.

00:08:15.728 --> 00:08:19.265 align:start size:84% position:16%
So the cultural
norm, I think becomes

00:08:19.265 --> 00:08:20.500
to be very, very careful

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:22.368
about being proud
of your success,

00:08:22.368 --> 00:08:25.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
about showing off any
pride, any of that at all.

00:08:25.271 --> 00:08:26.906
And the default

00:08:26.906 --> 00:08:30.042
becomes taken over by
the left at some point.

00:08:30.042 --> 00:08:33.112
I don't know exactly how the
woke people got in charge here

00:08:33.112 --> 00:08:34.981
in so many places,
it's partially 'cause
they're so loud.

00:08:34.981 --> 00:08:36.482
I think there's 5 or
10% of people here

00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:38.017 align:start size:81% position:19%
who really believe
in that stuff.

00:08:38.017 --> 00:08:39.252
And they've gotten very good.

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:40.453 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think it came from
the universities.

00:08:40.453 --> 00:08:41.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
You look at our
big tech companies,

00:08:41.888 --> 00:08:44.090 align:start position:0%
the culture at Google is really
modeled off of Stanford's.

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:46.659
It's all the PhDs, same thing
with a lot of the other ones.

00:08:46.659 --> 00:08:48.861 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so these tech companies
are basically taking a culture

00:08:48.861 --> 00:08:52.798 align:start size:97% position:3%
from a university that's been
broken for a few decades.

00:08:52.798 --> 00:08:55.735 align:start position:0%
And these woke people are
really good at punishing people

00:08:55.735 --> 00:08:57.270
who stand up to them
and trying to get them.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.071
And so it's just not worth
people's time to push back

00:08:59.071 --> 00:09:01.307
and so, if you're just
trying to do your job,

00:09:01.307 --> 00:09:04.644
you're just doing what you can.

00:09:04.644 --> 00:09:08.080
And these people get to be
in charge. It's really bad.

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.149 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So how do you run
companies then, and make sure

00:09:10.149 --> 00:09:13.286 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they don't get
infected by these ideas?

00:09:13.286 --> 00:09:15.588 align:start size:97% position:3%
- It's really important
who you hire, first of all.

00:09:15.588 --> 00:09:17.924 align:start size:97% position:3%
A lot of our companies,
you gotta be really careful

00:09:17.924 --> 00:09:19.091
who you let into your company.

00:09:19.091 --> 00:09:21.193 align:start size:88% position:13%
You do not want a
social justice activist

00:09:21.193 --> 00:09:23.362 align:start size:84% position:16%
who's gonna be
stirring things up.

00:09:23.362 --> 00:09:26.032 align:start size:84% position:16%
I loved what the CEO
of Coinbase, Brian
Armstrong put out

00:09:26.032 --> 00:09:28.467
the other day that
a lot of us shared.

00:09:28.467 --> 00:09:30.102
Our company is not about
all these other things.

00:09:30.102 --> 00:09:31.270
It's about our mission.

00:09:32.772 --> 00:09:34.307
There's other places you can go,

00:09:34.307 --> 00:09:36.943
if you want to focus on other
missions, this is our mission.

00:09:36.943 --> 00:09:40.079
So a lot of my companies, we
spend a decent amount of time

00:09:40.079 --> 00:09:41.914 align:start size:84% position:16%
just being really
careful who we let in.

00:09:41.914 --> 00:09:44.917 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I have to teach
CEOs, this is your job.

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:46.919 align:start size:84% position:16%
Don't come to me a
year from now and say,

00:09:46.919 --> 00:09:48.688
this is so horrible, these
people are so distracting.

00:09:48.688 --> 00:09:51.023
They're pushing us to do
all these stupid things.

00:09:51.023 --> 00:09:52.425
That would have been your fault

00:09:52.425 --> 00:09:53.893
for not figuring out,
and for hiring them.

00:09:53.893 --> 00:09:55.328
So this is something

00:09:55.328 --> 00:09:55.995 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of people are
learning about right now.

00:09:55.995 --> 00:09:56.929
- It's funny.

00:09:56.929 --> 00:09:58.164
I always find it interesting

00:09:58.164 --> 00:10:01.434 align:start size:88% position:13%
how it deviates from the
core mission, always.

00:10:01.434 --> 00:10:04.070
Every single commercial
you watch these days,

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:06.339
it's a company, but it's
not about their product.

00:10:06.339 --> 00:10:08.908 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's about what quote,
unquote, good they're doing.

00:10:08.908 --> 00:10:12.011
The only company that I
know, like major company,

00:10:12.011 --> 00:10:14.647
that's not doing it is T-Mobile.

00:10:14.647 --> 00:10:16.415 align:start size:88% position:13%
All their commercials
are about their product.

00:10:16.415 --> 00:10:19.385
And I'm always like, oh, I
know what they're selling,

00:10:19.385 --> 00:10:21.887 align:start size:88% position:13%
it's incredible!
- That's a real company.

00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:25.324 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think there is a role
for consumer brands

00:10:25.324 --> 00:10:27.126 align:start size:88% position:13%
to stand for values
that people believe in.

00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:29.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think in a free market,
people could choose

00:10:29.395 --> 00:10:30.563 align:start size:81% position:19%
to put their money
towards brands

00:10:30.563 --> 00:10:31.998 align:start size:84% position:16%
that go towards things
they believe in.

00:10:31.998 --> 00:10:34.400 align:start position:0%
And if it was purely reflecting
the desires of the market,

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.935
I think that's interesting.

00:10:35.935 --> 00:10:37.336
I think the problem is a
lot of these big companies

00:10:37.336 --> 00:10:38.604 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the marketing
departments are conquered

00:10:38.604 --> 00:10:40.973
by people with these
extremely warped views

00:10:40.973 --> 00:10:42.375
that do not reflect
their customers.

00:10:42.375 --> 00:10:43.976 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that becomes a
really weird disconnect

00:10:43.976 --> 00:10:46.178 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I'm hoping the market
will solve for that eventually

00:10:46.178 --> 00:10:47.513
by pushing back on
some of these things.

00:10:47.513 --> 00:10:48.814
- Yeah. All right.

00:10:48.814 --> 00:10:50.082
So let's back up to
that poo-pooh moment

00:10:50.082 --> 00:10:51.784 align:start size:81% position:19%
when you poo-pooed
the 22 billion.

00:10:53.853 --> 00:10:56.756
So Palentir went to IPO,
it's about 10 days ago,

00:10:56.756 --> 00:10:57.990
by the time we post this.

00:10:59.392 --> 00:11:01.093
I've had Peter on as you know,

00:11:01.093 --> 00:11:04.063 align:start size:88% position:13%
but can you just explain
what Palentir is?

00:11:04.063 --> 00:11:07.333
I feel like Palentir gets a
lot of really bizarre press

00:11:07.333 --> 00:11:10.336 align:start size:94% position:6%
and people have no concept
of what it actually is.

00:11:10.336 --> 00:11:13.339
- It's because it is a pretty
complicated thing to explain.

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:15.408 align:start size:81% position:19%
The best way I
like to look at it

00:11:15.408 --> 00:11:18.244 align:start position:0%
is we're taking things that
used to be done by IT services.

00:11:18.244 --> 00:11:20.279 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we started off
in the defense world

00:11:20.279 --> 00:11:21.414
and the intelligence world.

00:11:21.414 --> 00:11:22.682
And the US government

00:11:22.682 --> 00:11:25.184
spends $40 billion a
year gathering data.

00:11:25.184 --> 00:11:27.887
And then they spend a
similar amount of money

00:11:27.887 --> 00:11:30.856 align:start position:0%
paying IT service professionals
to try to build systems,

00:11:30.856 --> 00:11:32.425 align:start size:84% position:16%
to let the analysts
see the data,

00:11:32.425 --> 00:11:33.859
let them share it, et cetera.

00:11:33.859 --> 00:11:36.295 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it's a mess. Their
projects go way over budget.

00:11:36.295 --> 00:11:37.363
They waste billions of dollars

00:11:37.363 --> 00:11:38.664
as you'd expect on these things.

00:11:38.664 --> 00:11:39.899 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're paying
people to do things

00:11:39.899 --> 00:11:42.268
the same way they did
in the 1980s, 1990s.

00:11:42.268 --> 00:11:44.470
And so looking at
this after 9/11,

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:45.671
we said, wait a second here.

00:11:45.671 --> 00:11:46.972
The technology in Silicon Valley

00:11:46.972 --> 00:11:49.775
has gotten way ahead of
everything DC is doing.

00:11:49.775 --> 00:11:51.677
That was a sad thing
for us to realize.

00:11:51.677 --> 00:11:53.179
Because when you're
a computer scientist,

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:54.914
you look at the NSA in the 1970s

00:11:54.914 --> 00:11:56.682
and they were way ahead
of everyone else by far.

00:11:56.682 --> 00:11:58.350
So it used to be the US
government was way ahead.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:00.052
It's not anymore, no question.

00:12:00.052 --> 00:12:03.122 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so we said, how
can we build a platform

00:12:03.122 --> 00:12:04.857
that out of the box
replaced the need

00:12:04.857 --> 00:12:06.092 align:start size:81% position:19%
for the tens of
billions of spend?

00:12:06.092 --> 00:12:08.294
So our platforms is an
information environment,

00:12:08.294 --> 00:12:10.396
where you could take in
data from tons of places,

00:12:10.396 --> 00:12:12.698 align:start size:97% position:3%
to process it, it's very,
very expensive and hard to do

00:12:12.698 --> 00:12:14.934 align:start size:88% position:13%
and takes a very long
time, and then secure it

00:12:14.934 --> 00:12:16.936
and make sure only people who
are allowed to can see it.

00:12:16.936 --> 00:12:18.137 align:start size:84% position:16%
And then allow
people to build tools

00:12:18.137 --> 00:12:20.172
on top of it to collaborate.

00:12:20.172 --> 00:12:22.708
So for example, we help
intelligence agencies

00:12:22.708 --> 00:12:24.009
around the world collaborate.

00:12:24.009 --> 00:12:25.611
We help aerospace companies

00:12:25.611 --> 00:12:27.146 align:start size:84% position:16%
and their suppliers
collaborate with data.

00:12:27.146 --> 00:12:29.248
So it basically helps
you use all the data

00:12:30.116 --> 00:12:31.517
you're allowed to use

00:12:31.517 --> 00:12:33.619 align:start position:0%
and only people who are supposed
to see things, see them.

00:12:33.619 --> 00:12:35.054
It's a hard IT problem.

00:12:35.054 --> 00:12:38.090 align:start position:0%
- So for more libertarian
minded guys, like you and Peter,

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:41.160
does that cause a little
bit of an ethical dilemma?

00:12:41.160 --> 00:12:42.895 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're working with
the government!

00:12:44.964 --> 00:12:46.398 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well there's two
different things there.

00:12:46.398 --> 00:12:48.901 align:start position:0%
First of all, what was happening
before we built Palantir,

00:12:48.901 --> 00:12:50.536
was they were building
systems to see things

00:12:50.536 --> 00:12:53.172
with basically very
little safeguards.

00:12:53.172 --> 00:12:55.241
Imagined Jack Bauer
in the show "24,"

00:12:55.241 --> 00:12:56.542
and there's a terrorist.

00:12:56.542 --> 00:12:57.710
He'd just going to
go in the database

00:12:57.710 --> 00:12:59.178 align:start size:84% position:16%
and take the fricking
data he needs

00:12:59.178 --> 00:13:01.981 align:start size:97% position:3%
because otherwise someone's
going to die, right?

00:13:01.981 --> 00:13:03.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
A lot of people were like,
screw civil liberties.

00:13:03.315 --> 00:13:04.817 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're just gonna
take whatever we can.

00:13:04.817 --> 00:13:06.485 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so as civil
libertarians, we said, no,

00:13:06.485 --> 00:13:07.820
we're going to build Palantir.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:09.388
It's going to give them
more powerful access,

00:13:09.388 --> 00:13:11.223
but it's also going to be
able to make audit trails.

00:13:11.223 --> 00:13:12.491 align:start size:84% position:16%
We're gonna know
exactly who did what,

00:13:12.491 --> 00:13:14.126
and we're going to
enforce the rules.

00:13:14.126 --> 00:13:15.694
And whatever the rules are,
we're going to enforce them.

00:13:15.694 --> 00:13:17.129
We don't make the rules.

00:13:17.129 --> 00:13:19.465 align:start position:0%
President Obama makes the rules,
President Bush made rules.

00:13:19.465 --> 00:13:21.467 align:start size:84% position:16%
We were going to
enforce whatever the
rule, Congress does,

00:13:21.467 --> 00:13:23.035 align:start size:84% position:16%
we're gonna reinforce
those rules.

00:13:24.703 --> 00:13:27.072 align:start size:97% position:3%
Actually, Palantir is like the
most awesome privacy engine

00:13:27.072 --> 00:13:29.108 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the world because
we've really enforced it.

00:13:29.108 --> 00:13:30.709
If you use Palantir,
it's very hard for you

00:13:30.709 --> 00:13:32.711
to get away with anything
you're not supposed to do.

00:13:32.711 --> 00:13:34.446 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was something we
were really heartened by.

00:13:34.446 --> 00:13:35.881
The second one's an
interesting question.

00:13:35.881 --> 00:13:39.518 align:start size:88% position:13%
I built a lot of
things now in gov tech.

00:13:39.518 --> 00:13:41.120 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think for me, it's
part of being a realist.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:43.122 align:start size:84% position:16%
You asked about
being a libertarian,

00:13:43.122 --> 00:13:45.925 align:start size:84% position:16%
Cato is the most
famous libertarian in
the Roman Republic.

00:13:45.925 --> 00:13:47.827
And what Cato would would do

00:13:47.827 --> 00:13:49.662 align:start size:81% position:19%
is he wouldn't
compromise at all.

00:13:49.662 --> 00:13:51.664
So he's really admired
for being uncompromising

00:13:51.664 --> 00:13:53.732 align:start size:88% position:13%
and constantly fighting
for what was right.

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:55.267 align:start size:84% position:16%
But because he
wouldn't compromise,

00:13:55.267 --> 00:13:57.570
he wouldn't work with Pompei.
Pompei went over to Caesar.

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:58.771
They eventually
lost the Republic

00:13:58.771 --> 00:14:00.105
because he was so stubborn.

00:14:00.105 --> 00:14:01.307
He couldn't get things done

00:14:01.307 --> 00:14:02.575 align:start size:84% position:16%
and therefore you
lost the entire thing.

00:14:02.575 --> 00:14:04.076
And the entire thing was gone.

00:14:04.076 --> 00:14:06.846
So as much as I admire Cato,
and I have a lot of Cato in me

00:14:06.846 --> 00:14:08.647
as I've grown up, I'm a realist.

00:14:08.647 --> 00:14:11.150
And so I want to fight for my
values as well as possible.

00:14:11.150 --> 00:14:13.485
But if the government's
gonna be doing something,

00:14:13.485 --> 00:14:16.488 line:0%
let's actually help it reflect
our values of a free society,

00:14:16.488 --> 00:14:17.723 line:0%
as much as possible.

00:14:17.723 --> 00:14:18.891 line:0%
Let's helping not
waste billions,

00:14:18.891 --> 00:14:20.159 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
let's help it protect
civil liberties.

00:14:20.159 --> 00:14:21.694 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
Let's help it actually
get the terrorists,

00:14:21.694 --> 00:14:24.864 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the worst of all
worlds is that the government

00:14:24.864 --> 00:14:26.999
is doing all these things and
it's viciously incompetent.

00:14:26.999 --> 00:14:28.634
I think that's probably
really bad for everyone.

00:14:28.634 --> 00:14:30.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, you're
speaking my language.

00:14:30.202 --> 00:14:32.104
So can you explain
to me then as someone

00:14:32.104 --> 00:14:33.505
that has worked with
the US government,

00:14:33.505 --> 00:14:36.308
why are there so many
government websites

00:14:36.308 --> 00:14:40.045 align:start size:88% position:13%
that are so ridiculously
old and broken?

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.216 align:start size:97% position:3%
I always use the example,
and you as a California guy,

00:14:44.216 --> 00:14:47.152 align:start size:88% position:13%
I pay my California
State taxes on a website

00:14:47.152 --> 00:14:50.990
that literally looks like
it's Prodigy from 1999.

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:52.591
Why is that?

00:14:52.591 --> 00:14:53.893 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Dave, there's
no accountability.

00:14:53.893 --> 00:14:55.361
And there's no incentives.

00:14:55.361 --> 00:14:57.696 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the people who win
these government contracts

00:14:57.696 --> 00:15:00.599 align:start position:0%
are working through these really
byzantine procurement rules

00:15:00.599 --> 00:15:02.434 align:start size:94% position:6%
that favor the incumbents,
and favor of the people

00:15:02.434 --> 00:15:04.236 align:start size:94% position:6%
that have paid for massive
amounts of lobbying.

00:15:04.236 --> 00:15:06.238 align:start size:88% position:13%
They've been doing it
the same way since 1987,

00:15:06.238 --> 00:15:07.973
or whenever they started
building these things,

00:15:07.973 --> 00:15:10.542
probably '97 for that
website, you know?

00:15:10.542 --> 00:15:12.011
So what we need in government

00:15:12.011 --> 00:15:15.514 align:start size:97% position:3%
is we need to completely
change our procurement works

00:15:15.514 --> 00:15:18.284 align:start size:97% position:3%
and we need to have
accountability and how metrics

00:15:18.284 --> 00:15:19.885
and all the same things
you do in a business.

00:15:19.885 --> 00:15:21.453
In my businesses, if
we have a website,

00:15:21.453 --> 00:15:23.822 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's certain rules for
measuring how well it's doing

00:15:23.822 --> 00:15:26.825 align:start size:94% position:6%
and measuring the feedback
and NPS scores, right?

00:15:26.825 --> 00:15:29.028
An NPS score is when you
get how much people like it

00:15:29.028 --> 00:15:30.562 align:start size:88% position:13%
and how much they
recommend it to friends.

00:15:30.562 --> 00:15:33.499
There's things like this, the
government should have to do.

00:15:33.499 --> 00:15:34.967
The biggest problem

00:15:34.967 --> 00:15:35.901 align:start size:94% position:6%
is government has special
interests everywhere.

00:15:35.901 --> 00:15:37.336
Especially government unions

00:15:37.336 --> 00:15:39.438
that refuse to let them
be held accountable.

00:15:39.438 --> 00:15:42.007
So until we can hold our
governments accountable,

00:15:42.007 --> 00:15:44.343 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's gonna keep being broken.
- Yeah. All right.

00:15:44.343 --> 00:15:45.945
I want to shift a little
bit because as you know,

00:15:45.945 --> 00:15:48.013
we're doing Locals Week here.

00:15:48.013 --> 00:15:51.517 align:start size:97% position:3%
When I created Locals and
when I had the idea of Locals

00:15:51.517 --> 00:15:53.319
and I brought in some
of the team for Locals,

00:15:53.319 --> 00:15:54.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
we had to raise a
little bit of dough,

00:15:54.787 --> 00:15:58.924
and I started asking around,
your name just kept popping up.

00:15:58.924 --> 00:16:01.026 align:start size:97% position:3%
People just kept saying, this
sounds like Joe Lonsdale,

00:16:01.026 --> 00:16:02.561
you gotta talk to Joe Lonsdale.

00:16:03.963 --> 00:16:06.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm not even totally sure
how we got connected.

00:16:07.433 --> 00:16:09.768 align:start size:84% position:16%
We jumped on a
Zoom or on a Skype.

00:16:09.768 --> 00:16:12.204 align:start size:97% position:3%
And in the first conversation
within five minutes,

00:16:12.204 --> 00:16:13.706
you were like, yeah,

00:16:13.706 --> 00:16:14.974
I want to throw you guys
some money and good luck.

00:16:14.974 --> 00:16:17.242 align:start size:88% position:13%
And you've helped guide
us with this thing.

00:16:17.242 --> 00:16:19.478
When someone approaches you,

00:16:20.913 --> 00:16:22.147
this doesn't have to be
about Local specifically,

00:16:22.147 --> 00:16:23.682
but generally when
people approach you

00:16:23.682 --> 00:16:26.485
with ideas like this, what
goes through the checklist

00:16:26.485 --> 00:16:29.154
in your mind of like, oh, this
is something I want to fund,

00:16:29.154 --> 00:16:30.589
or I see something here,

00:16:30.589 --> 00:16:32.992 align:start size:94% position:6%
or I'm gonna write a
massive check for this one

00:16:32.992 --> 00:16:34.293
or a little bit for this one.

00:16:34.293 --> 00:16:36.228
What is the whole process like?

00:16:36.228 --> 00:16:39.264
- Sure. 8VC is my venture fund.

00:16:39.264 --> 00:16:41.467 align:start size:97% position:3%
We run a multi-billion
dollar venture capital firm.

00:16:41.467 --> 00:16:43.502
There's 40 people, 9 partners.

00:16:43.502 --> 00:16:46.438 align:start size:97% position:3%
And at 8VC, we have different
themes we're focused on.

00:16:46.438 --> 00:16:48.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the big question there
is what's possible now

00:16:48.774 --> 00:16:51.010 align:start size:84% position:16%
that was not possible
five years ago?

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:52.478
That's the big question.

00:16:52.478 --> 00:16:54.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we look for really
taught technology cultures

00:16:54.346 --> 00:16:56.548
and tech talent and help
nurture and build those.

00:16:56.548 --> 00:16:58.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then we look for them
working on these problems

00:16:58.517 --> 00:17:00.052 align:start size:88% position:13%
that are newly
possible, because again,

00:17:00.052 --> 00:17:01.954
the job of venture capital
is to evolve the economy.

00:17:01.954 --> 00:17:03.822
So you have a lot of
things in that vein.

00:17:03.822 --> 00:17:06.692 align:start size:97% position:3%
I also do a much smaller
amount of personal investing

00:17:06.692 --> 00:17:08.594
when something is just
really important to me,

00:17:08.594 --> 00:17:10.062
ideologically, or for the world,

00:17:10.062 --> 00:17:13.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
even if it doesn't fit
exactly into my VC bucket.

00:17:13.365 --> 00:17:15.000
I love to support things
I think are very important

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:16.468 align:start size:88% position:13%
for the world, like
what you guys are doing.

00:17:16.468 --> 00:17:17.736
- Yeah.

00:17:17.736 --> 00:17:20.539
Do you sense that
the small internet

00:17:20.539 --> 00:17:22.474 align:start size:88% position:13%
or the bottom up
internet is the future?

00:17:22.474 --> 00:17:25.277
That we're gonna move away
from these big tech platforms

00:17:25.277 --> 00:17:26.979
and do things a little bit more

00:17:26.979 --> 00:17:28.380 align:start size:81% position:19%
like what we're
doing with Locals,

00:17:28.380 --> 00:17:30.682 align:start size:88% position:13%
where people will create
their own communities

00:17:30.682 --> 00:17:32.818
and figure out how to network up

00:17:32.818 --> 00:17:35.454
so it's really just flipping
the whole thing upside down?

00:17:35.454 --> 00:17:37.689 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I would love that
to be the case.

00:17:37.689 --> 00:17:41.093 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think in some cases
that will be what happens.

00:17:41.093 --> 00:17:43.629
One of the big challenges
that's unusual right now

00:17:43.629 --> 00:17:45.731
is in the old days, when
you think of a monopoly,

00:17:45.731 --> 00:17:47.099
you think of something that
gets to be somewhat decadent

00:17:47.099 --> 00:17:49.334
and starts to decline.

00:17:49.334 --> 00:17:51.937 align:start size:88% position:13%
And right now there
is, at least for today,

00:17:51.937 --> 00:17:53.472
an advantage that the
big companies have,

00:17:53.472 --> 00:17:56.175
is they keep attracting the
very, very, very top talent.

00:17:56.175 --> 00:17:57.743
So as much as I think
they're doing things

00:17:57.743 --> 00:18:00.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
that are morally and ethically
wrong, a big challenge,

00:18:00.312 --> 00:18:02.014
we're gonna face to bring
that vision you mentioned,

00:18:02.014 --> 00:18:04.416
which is possibly a good vision

00:18:04.416 --> 00:18:06.351 align:start size:84% position:16%
is how do we compete
for top talent?

00:18:06.351 --> 00:18:08.053
Because it's a very
unusual circumstance.

00:18:08.053 --> 00:18:09.254
They may be monopolies,

00:18:09.254 --> 00:18:11.590 align:start size:88% position:13%
but they are just hiring
all the top talent,

00:18:11.590 --> 00:18:13.158 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is very impressive
to still watch.

00:18:13.158 --> 00:18:15.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah. And it's like,
you you've got some dough.

00:18:15.761 --> 00:18:17.229
It's not like you can't compete,

00:18:17.229 --> 00:18:20.132
but they've got,
even more dough!

00:18:20.132 --> 00:18:21.600
- They have this money machine.

00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:24.236 align:start position:0%
People think Palantir being
worth $22 billion is something.

00:18:24.236 --> 00:18:25.871 align:start size:97% position:3%
Google has a money
machine, I think shoots off

00:18:25.871 --> 00:18:27.940
like three or four billion
of free cash flow a quarter.

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:30.709
It's just ridiculous how
much money these guys have.

00:18:30.709 --> 00:18:33.545
- Do you know what they're
actually making money on

00:18:33.545 --> 00:18:34.780
at this point?

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:36.048
I've heard a bunch of
people talk about this,

00:18:36.048 --> 00:18:38.117
that it's like, what
products do they have

00:18:38.117 --> 00:18:40.652
that are actually making money?

00:18:42.621 --> 00:18:43.822
- One of my companies

00:18:43.822 --> 00:18:45.791 align:start size:88% position:13%
actually funds a few
percent of their revenue

00:18:45.791 --> 00:18:47.025
for Google and Facebook.

00:18:47.025 --> 00:18:49.328 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's the number two
to Amazon called Wish.

00:18:49.328 --> 00:18:50.729
And we just advertise.

00:18:50.729 --> 00:18:52.431
And we do like Dollar
Store stuff online

00:18:52.431 --> 00:18:53.832
it's like having
a Walmart online.

00:18:53.832 --> 00:18:55.200
So there's things like that

00:18:55.200 --> 00:18:56.401
where we're selling
stuff to consumers

00:18:56.401 --> 00:18:58.003
and consumers are finding it.

00:18:58.003 --> 00:19:00.739
But there's basically
everything you sell,

00:19:00.739 --> 00:19:02.841
these guys make money
on, especially Google.

00:19:02.841 --> 00:19:04.376
Anything you might search for,

00:19:04.376 --> 00:19:06.278
anything you might
want to figure out

00:19:06.278 --> 00:19:08.347
Google's gotten to be very,
very good at monetizing.

00:19:08.347 --> 00:19:09.781
- Yeah.

00:19:09.781 --> 00:19:11.216 align:start size:97% position:3%
What do you think generally
about the subscription

00:19:11.216 --> 00:19:13.652
versus ad model these days?

00:19:13.652 --> 00:19:15.154
Is that changing
a little bit now

00:19:15.154 --> 00:19:17.789
that that people have woken
up to some of this stuff?

00:19:17.789 --> 00:19:19.158
- Yeah, I think that you said,

00:19:19.158 --> 00:19:20.425
I think for the bottom up world,

00:19:20.425 --> 00:19:21.627
you're gonna start
seeing a lot more

00:19:21.627 --> 00:19:23.562
of a subscription model
and how things work.

00:19:23.562 --> 00:19:24.930
I think that is how
media should work.

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:27.366
My favorite media,
I'm a subscriber to

00:19:27.366 --> 00:19:29.701 align:start size:81% position:19%
whether it's like,
the information

00:19:31.870 --> 00:19:34.573
is slightly woke, but it's
better than the other ones.

00:19:34.573 --> 00:19:36.975
It's hard to find
everything you get.

00:19:38.777 --> 00:19:41.413
I think a lot of stuff
you guys feature is,

00:19:41.413 --> 00:19:42.781
there's a lot of people on there

00:19:42.781 --> 00:19:45.384 align:start size:97% position:3%
who definitely we're
following and subscribing to.

00:19:45.384 --> 00:19:47.419 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think things are shifting
back to subscription

00:19:47.419 --> 00:19:49.788
because it's a healthier
thing, but we're gonna see,

00:19:49.788 --> 00:19:51.123
it hopefully get more
in that direction.

00:19:51.123 --> 00:19:52.558
- Yeah.

00:19:52.558 --> 00:19:54.793 align:start position:0%
So let's talk a little bit
about your your political life.

00:19:54.793 --> 00:19:57.496
So first off, for people that
have no idea what Cicero is,

00:19:57.496 --> 00:19:59.031
can you just explain
what they are first

00:19:59.031 --> 00:20:01.133
then we'll talk about your
involvement a little bit.

00:20:01.133 --> 00:20:02.601
- Yeah, sure.

00:20:02.601 --> 00:20:05.103
It kind of grew out of a
lot of my other activity

00:20:05.103 --> 00:20:06.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
where you you'd learn
about different industries

00:20:06.738 --> 00:20:08.473 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you'd say, wow,
this is really broken.

00:20:08.473 --> 00:20:10.776
I wonder why this
part of healthcare

00:20:10.776 --> 00:20:11.977
doesn't work this
much better way

00:20:11.977 --> 00:20:13.579 align:start size:88% position:13%
that would be like five
times as efficient?

00:20:13.579 --> 00:20:15.781 align:start size:97% position:3%
And sometimes you'll find
out, well, they need a system.

00:20:15.781 --> 00:20:16.982 align:start size:88% position:13%
We need to do something
as an entrepreneur

00:20:16.982 --> 00:20:18.283
and we'll build companies.

00:20:18.283 --> 00:20:19.585
But a lot of times you'll say,

00:20:19.585 --> 00:20:21.386
it's not because no
ones built a company,

00:20:21.386 --> 00:20:23.188 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's because the
policy is just wrong.

00:20:23.188 --> 00:20:24.690
It's like the policy here

00:20:24.690 --> 00:20:26.391
is causing healthcare to be
three times as expensive.

00:20:26.391 --> 00:20:28.527 align:start size:81% position:19%
The policy here
is causing housing

00:20:28.527 --> 00:20:30.896
to be twice as expensive as
it should be in this area.

00:20:30.896 --> 00:20:33.332
And so the more I
look at politics

00:20:33.332 --> 00:20:35.567
and the more I get
afraid of socialists,

00:20:35.567 --> 00:20:37.736
coming and convincing our
kids to be socialists,

00:20:37.736 --> 00:20:39.004
the more I've realized, wow,

00:20:39.004 --> 00:20:41.840 align:start size:84% position:16%
every area of our
society that's broken,

00:20:41.840 --> 00:20:44.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
it tends to be broken
because of big government.

00:20:44.243 --> 00:20:45.510
And because the best ideas

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:47.012
are not allowed to
compete and win.

00:20:47.012 --> 00:20:48.780 align:start size:97% position:3%
What you want in society,
the reason free society works

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:50.282
is if someone has a better idea,

00:20:50.282 --> 00:20:51.817
that idea wins out over time.

00:20:51.817 --> 00:20:54.219
This is a better way for us
to do probation and parole

00:20:54.219 --> 00:20:55.921
because less people
will go back to jail.

00:20:55.921 --> 00:20:57.789
You want a way for
that idea to win.

00:20:57.789 --> 00:20:59.391 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think a lot of people
don't understand this.

00:20:59.391 --> 00:21:00.592
They think that you
could just publish,

00:21:00.592 --> 00:21:01.960 align:start size:84% position:16%
this is a better
way to run a prison.

00:21:01.960 --> 00:21:03.228
And then other people
will just adopt it.

00:21:03.228 --> 00:21:04.763
And then society
will move forward.

00:21:04.763 --> 00:21:06.465
And it turns out that's
not how society works.

00:21:06.465 --> 00:21:07.799
That's a false model of society.

00:21:07.799 --> 00:21:09.167
The correct model of society

00:21:09.167 --> 00:21:10.802
is you have to have a
competition of ideas.

00:21:10.802 --> 00:21:12.271
You have to have accountability.

00:21:12.271 --> 00:21:14.873
You have to have something
that makes the bad ideas lose

00:21:14.873 --> 00:21:16.041
and the good ideas win.

00:21:16.041 --> 00:21:19.111 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so Cicero is helping
put forward laws.

00:21:19.111 --> 00:21:21.546
It's helping figure out where
we can take these best ideas

00:21:21.546 --> 00:21:23.415
and identify them
and create systems

00:21:23.415 --> 00:21:25.417 align:start size:88% position:13%
where the best ideas
will win in these areas.

00:21:25.417 --> 00:21:27.452
- Is the hardest part of
that just the messaging?

00:21:27.452 --> 00:21:29.988 align:start size:84% position:16%
Where I think most
people, unfortunately,

00:21:29.988 --> 00:21:32.391 align:start size:88% position:13%
they just see, oh,
something's screwed up.

00:21:32.391 --> 00:21:33.759
It just needs more money.

00:21:33.759 --> 00:21:36.194
That's just like the
one level thought.

00:21:36.194 --> 00:21:37.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I think it's a really
hard thing to teach people

00:21:37.663 --> 00:21:38.897
to think in terms of systems,

00:21:38.897 --> 00:21:42.601
most people are not
systems thinkers.

00:21:42.601 --> 00:21:45.604
A good leader might say,
this is a good program.

00:21:45.604 --> 00:21:46.872
I'm going to give it money.

00:21:46.872 --> 00:21:48.173
Whereas a great
leader would say,

00:21:48.173 --> 00:21:49.975
how do we redesign the system

00:21:49.975 --> 00:21:51.276
so that the good
things are winning

00:21:51.276 --> 00:21:52.978 align:start size:81% position:19%
and the bad things
are going away?

00:21:52.978 --> 00:21:57.182 align:start position:0%
The redesign the system problem
is not what people think.

00:21:57.182 --> 00:21:58.717
The market is a system.

00:21:58.717 --> 00:22:00.218 align:start size:94% position:6%
The market's one of the
greatest systems there is.

00:22:00.218 --> 00:22:02.654 align:start position:0%
And the more we can reflect
the better systems, the better.

00:22:02.654 --> 00:22:04.456 align:start size:97% position:3%
But you're right, people
don't understand it very well.

00:22:04.456 --> 00:22:06.358
- Right, can you give
me a couple examples

00:22:06.358 --> 00:22:08.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
of the type of things,
let's talk about healthcare.

00:22:08.694 --> 00:22:11.697
What can we do to clear
up this healthcare mess?

00:22:12.998 --> 00:22:16.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
Give me your presidential
debate platform.

00:22:16.034 --> 00:22:18.403 align:start size:84% position:16%
- There's so many
things we're pushing.

00:22:18.403 --> 00:22:20.038
There's a group called PBMs.

00:22:20.038 --> 00:22:22.040
Three of the Fortune
20 companies are PBMs,

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:23.575
pharmacy benefit manufacturers.

00:22:23.575 --> 00:22:25.510 align:start size:84% position:16%
They're the guys
who tell pharmacists

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:27.212
what drugs they could sell and
make them available to them.

00:22:27.212 --> 00:22:29.548 align:start size:97% position:3%
They're kind of the middlemen
on drugs in America.

00:22:29.548 --> 00:22:32.150 align:start size:97% position:3%
And they do all these things
where they gag everyone.

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:34.953 align:start position:0%
And so one thing we stopped
them from doing, which was good.

00:22:34.953 --> 00:22:36.421
we finally passed it in
the Senate two years ago,

00:22:36.421 --> 00:22:38.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was very, very involved
putting this forward

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:39.891
is they used to say,
okay, pharmacists,

00:22:39.891 --> 00:22:42.094 align:start size:84% position:16%
if there's two
generic drug options,

00:22:42.094 --> 00:22:44.730
even if one is, 20 times
cheaper than the other,

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:46.531
if the doctor prescribes
the more expensive one,

00:22:46.531 --> 00:22:49.101 align:start size:88% position:13%
you're not allowed to
tell him or the patient,

00:22:49.101 --> 00:22:50.669 align:start position:0%
you can't tell her or the
patient about the cheaper ones.

00:22:50.669 --> 00:22:51.703
Totally crazy, right?

00:22:54.339 --> 00:22:56.108 align:start size:84% position:16%
So we got rid of that.
That's illegal now.

00:22:56.108 --> 00:22:57.976 align:start size:81% position:19%
- What was the
logic behind that?

00:22:57.976 --> 00:22:59.778 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's just purely because
they're in the pocket

00:22:59.778 --> 00:23:00.912 align:start size:94% position:6%
of big pharma?
- It's just their cartel.

00:23:00.912 --> 00:23:02.114
It's their cartel power.

00:23:02.114 --> 00:23:03.448
They like being able
to make tons more money

00:23:03.448 --> 00:23:04.983 align:start size:84% position:16%
using their cartel.
- Yeah.

00:23:04.983 --> 00:23:06.451
- They say we're not gonna
give you these other drugs

00:23:06.451 --> 00:23:08.687
only we have access to
because we're this cartel

00:23:08.687 --> 00:23:11.390 align:start position:0%
and it's really hard
regulatorily to compete with us

00:23:11.390 --> 00:23:13.225
unless you don't
tell these things.

00:23:13.225 --> 00:23:14.760 align:start size:81% position:19%
But here's another
thing they do.

00:23:14.760 --> 00:23:15.994
They don't let people tell
the prices they're paying.

00:23:15.994 --> 00:23:18.497
So pharmacists would
love to be able to say,

00:23:18.497 --> 00:23:22.401 align:start position:0%
hey, other PBM, upstart, here's
what I'm paying on these.

00:23:23.835 --> 00:23:25.670
Can you give me a better
deal? And that's illegal.

00:23:25.670 --> 00:23:26.505
You're not allowed to.

00:23:26.505 --> 00:23:27.739
Prices are trade secret,

00:23:27.739 --> 00:23:29.608
according to these
cartels in America.

00:23:29.608 --> 00:23:31.243
And there's lots of parts
of healthcare like this,

00:23:31.243 --> 00:23:32.978
where all the prices
are trade secrets,

00:23:32.978 --> 00:23:34.413
and they won't tell
you ahead of time

00:23:34.413 --> 00:23:37.182 align:start size:97% position:3%
because that stops competition
from coming in on them.

00:23:37.182 --> 00:23:38.850 align:start size:94% position:6%
Insurance companies would
love to be able to go

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:41.219
to other providers and say,
these people are charging us

00:23:41.219 --> 00:23:42.387
this much over here.

00:23:42.387 --> 00:23:45.023
Can we pay a lot less?

00:23:45.023 --> 00:23:47.125 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's all these gag orders
where you can't do that,

00:23:47.125 --> 00:23:48.727
it's against the rules.

00:23:48.727 --> 00:23:51.296
In my view, in order for
free market to work better,

00:23:51.296 --> 00:23:52.597
you want competition.

00:23:52.597 --> 00:23:55.133
And therefore you want
to ban these gag orders.

00:23:55.133 --> 00:23:57.502
Gag orders are something
only cartels would want.

00:23:57.502 --> 00:23:59.037
It's amazing, Dave,

00:23:59.037 --> 00:24:01.072
you go to Congress and talk
to senators and they say,

00:24:01.072 --> 00:24:03.241 align:start position:0%
I've had all these people
visit me from the pharma lobby.

00:24:03.241 --> 00:24:05.177
And they've explained to me

00:24:05.177 --> 00:24:06.778
that if this thing
was transparent,

00:24:06.778 --> 00:24:09.014
everyone would collude
and prices will go up.

00:24:10.649 --> 00:24:12.818 align:start size:97% position:3%
I was like, okay guys, they're
paying billions of dollars

00:24:12.818 --> 00:24:16.087
to convince you of this, right?

00:24:16.087 --> 00:24:17.589
That if it was transparent,

00:24:17.589 --> 00:24:18.924
it would make the markets
not functioning as well.

00:24:18.924 --> 00:24:21.426
This is the senators
on both sides, man.

00:24:21.426 --> 00:24:23.929
I love working on issues, and
obviously believe in freedom

00:24:23.929 --> 00:24:25.363
and believe in free markets.

00:24:25.363 --> 00:24:26.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
But there are people
bought off in this country

00:24:26.832 --> 00:24:28.300
on both sides by these lobbyists

00:24:28.300 --> 00:24:30.602
who are not putting in
the pro-market solutions.

00:24:30.602 --> 00:24:32.337
And so we're working hard on it.

00:24:32.337 --> 00:24:34.272
I'm optimistic we're gonna win.

00:24:34.272 --> 00:24:36.174
But in every one of these areas,

00:24:36.174 --> 00:24:38.510
there's just stuff like this
that just drives me crazy.

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:39.644
- It's kinda hilarious.

00:24:39.644 --> 00:24:41.112 align:start size:84% position:16%
You go into a
congressman's office.

00:24:41.112 --> 00:24:43.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
They tell you that if they
take the secrecy away,

00:24:43.715 --> 00:24:47.552
and then everyone can actually
negotiate, prices will go up.

00:24:47.552 --> 00:24:50.155 align:start size:97% position:3%
They definitely missed
something in economics 101.

00:24:50.155 --> 00:24:51.423
- I've literally had
conversations like this

00:24:51.423 --> 00:24:53.024
with people on both parties.

00:24:53.024 --> 00:24:54.359 align:start size:84% position:16%
And there are people
in both parties

00:24:54.359 --> 00:24:56.528 align:start size:81% position:19%
who are on the
good guy side too.

00:24:56.528 --> 00:24:57.896
It drives me crazy.

00:24:57.896 --> 00:25:00.432
- Do you find the
partisan gridlock?

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:04.302 align:start size:88% position:13%
Is it 50/50? is one side
worse than the other?

00:25:05.670 --> 00:25:07.272 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think generally speaking,
people on the right

00:25:07.272 --> 00:25:08.773
are a little more open
to free market stuff,

00:25:08.773 --> 00:25:10.242
but is it pretty close?

00:25:10.242 --> 00:25:11.610 align:start position:0%
- People in their right are
more open to free market stuff.

00:25:11.610 --> 00:25:13.345 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the Freedom
Caucus, for example,

00:25:13.345 --> 00:25:15.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
while I admire a lot of them,
a lot of them are Cato's

00:25:15.547 --> 00:25:17.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
where the thing
that I said earlier,

00:25:17.048 --> 00:25:18.416
where they're so extreme,

00:25:18.416 --> 00:25:19.684 align:start size:88% position:13%
that they won't even
compromise a little bit.

00:25:19.684 --> 00:25:21.486
It's interesting in
the Bush White House,

00:25:21.486 --> 00:25:23.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
I have a lot of
admiration for Dick Cheney

00:25:23.688 --> 00:25:25.090
on a lot of issues.

00:25:25.090 --> 00:25:27.926 align:start position:0%
But Dick Cheney was so stubborn
as a Cato on this issue

00:25:27.926 --> 00:25:29.594
that he didn't want to
require transparency.

00:25:29.594 --> 00:25:31.530 align:start position:0%
'Cause he said the market
should be able to figure it out.

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:33.932
We shouldn't have to
require transparency.

00:25:33.932 --> 00:25:35.500
But all these other
healthcare analysts,

00:25:35.500 --> 00:25:38.036
including myself say,
it's pretty obvious

00:25:38.036 --> 00:25:39.271
that you do need
transparency here

00:25:39.271 --> 00:25:40.772
'cause that's the only
way it's gonna work.

00:25:40.772 --> 00:25:43.842
And there's so many little
mini monopolies and whatnot,

00:25:43.842 --> 00:25:45.076 align:start size:81% position:19%
it makes them more
like a market,

00:25:45.076 --> 00:25:47.012
more like a free society
with more competition.

00:25:47.012 --> 00:25:48.513
And he was afraid to
impose transparency

00:25:48.513 --> 00:25:50.749 align:start size:97% position:3%
because he thought that was
telling the market what to do.

00:25:50.749 --> 00:25:52.217
So it's interesting.

00:25:52.217 --> 00:25:53.652 align:start position:0%
You have people on the far right
who are pretty intransigent

00:25:53.652 --> 00:25:55.020 align:start size:84% position:16%
sometimes about some
of these things.

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:56.254
- Yeah.

00:25:56.254 --> 00:25:57.455
What else should
we be thinking of

00:25:57.455 --> 00:26:00.225 align:start size:84% position:16%
as the big issues
related to healthcare?

00:26:00.225 --> 00:26:02.227 align:start size:88% position:13%
Like, okay, prescription
drug prices,

00:26:02.227 --> 00:26:03.929
what are the other types
of things that you work on?

00:26:03.929 --> 00:26:06.097
- The other really big one,

00:26:06.097 --> 00:26:07.599 align:start size:84% position:16%
so what you look
for, in these spaces,

00:26:07.599 --> 00:26:09.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
you look for where all the
contribution margin is,

00:26:09.301 --> 00:26:11.002 align:start size:88% position:13%
what contribution
margin is in businesses,

00:26:11.002 --> 00:26:12.571
you know what it is, of course.

00:26:12.571 --> 00:26:15.173 align:start size:97% position:3%
It's how much money
businesses are actually making

00:26:16.374 --> 00:26:17.809 align:start size:81% position:19%
on the services
they're providing.

00:26:17.809 --> 00:26:20.278
And the biggest area
of contribution margin

00:26:20.278 --> 00:26:21.546
is health providers.

00:26:21.546 --> 00:26:22.714
Health providers are
hospitals basically,

00:26:22.714 --> 00:26:24.482
or things like hospitals.

00:26:24.482 --> 00:26:26.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's really egregious
how this works.

00:26:26.451 --> 00:26:27.919
And it's tough

00:26:27.919 --> 00:26:29.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause no one wants to be
against their local hospital.

00:26:29.187 --> 00:26:30.455
It sounds like the thing
you should give money to

00:26:30.455 --> 00:26:32.490
and support, but a
lot of hospitals,

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:33.792
Medicare and Medicaid literally

00:26:33.792 --> 00:26:35.360 align:start size:84% position:16%
will pay two to
three times as much

00:26:35.360 --> 00:26:38.096
for the exact same service
to a hospital versus a clinic

00:26:38.096 --> 00:26:39.864
because the hospitals
have so much power

00:26:39.864 --> 00:26:41.399 align:start size:84% position:16%
and they've set
things up that way.

00:26:41.399 --> 00:26:43.134
And they say, oh, we
have to get paid more

00:26:43.134 --> 00:26:44.402
because have more expenses.

00:26:44.402 --> 00:26:45.904
And I'm thinking, wait
a second, in a market,

00:26:45.904 --> 00:26:47.372
you don't want to have to
pay three times as much,

00:26:47.372 --> 00:26:49.608
for the same exact outcome,
or even a worse outcome.

00:26:49.608 --> 00:26:52.277 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's really interesting
if you map it out,

00:26:52.277 --> 00:26:53.478
there's all these health systems

00:26:53.478 --> 00:26:55.547 align:start size:88% position:13%
that have bought up lots
of things in an area

00:26:55.547 --> 00:26:56.848
to give themselves
more pricing power,

00:26:56.848 --> 00:26:58.583 align:start size:84% position:16%
because if they
buy the one nearby,

00:26:58.583 --> 00:27:00.118
then you can't have competition.

00:27:00.118 --> 00:27:02.120
You can't actually have an
insurance company that says,

00:27:02.120 --> 00:27:03.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
well, we're only going to
work with this reasonable one,

00:27:03.521 --> 00:27:04.623
not this other one.

00:27:04.623 --> 00:27:06.157
And it's really bad.

00:27:06.157 --> 00:27:07.626
And here's the thing
that's really crazy.

00:27:07.626 --> 00:27:09.828
A lot of these health
systems are nonprofits.

00:27:09.828 --> 00:27:11.896
And so if you say,
oh, it's a nonprofit,

00:27:11.896 --> 00:27:14.332
that means it must be a
good guy for the world.

00:27:14.332 --> 00:27:15.533
You can't do anything.

00:27:15.533 --> 00:27:17.002 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so the FTC, the
Federal Trade Commission

00:27:17.002 --> 00:27:18.970
is not allowed to look
at nonprofits right now.

00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:20.972 align:start size:97% position:3%
So you have all these
things have gone nonprofit,

00:27:20.972 --> 00:27:23.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
have bought up massive
amounts of an area,

00:27:23.808 --> 00:27:25.477
and then can jack up
the prices and a lot.

00:27:25.477 --> 00:27:26.711 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the people
running the nonprofit

00:27:26.711 --> 00:27:28.246
are making huge
amounts of money,

00:27:28.246 --> 00:27:29.981 align:start size:88% position:13%
even though it's
officially a non-profit,

00:27:29.981 --> 00:27:31.816
and it's a big problem.

00:27:31.816 --> 00:27:34.986 align:start size:84% position:16%
One of the big
things is antitrust

00:27:34.986 --> 00:27:38.690 align:start position:0%
and letting the FTC do antitrust
against nonprofits as well.

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:39.824
And really what it's about

00:27:39.824 --> 00:27:41.292 align:start size:84% position:16%
is it's about
creating competition.

00:27:41.292 --> 00:27:43.495 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's about putting markets
back into place here.

00:27:43.495 --> 00:27:44.729
- Yeah.

00:27:44.729 --> 00:27:46.197
How do you decide
when the government

00:27:46.197 --> 00:27:48.233
can get involved there, the
libertarian side of you?

00:27:48.233 --> 00:27:49.467
Well, I guess that's
what you're saying

00:27:49.467 --> 00:27:51.202
about being a realist
as opposed to a purist.

00:27:51.202 --> 00:27:53.204
- You have to be a realist.

00:27:54.172 --> 00:27:55.607
And the libertarian thing here

00:27:55.607 --> 00:27:57.375 align:start size:94% position:6%
is make it easier to
start competitors, right?

00:27:57.375 --> 00:27:59.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
Make it easier to start
health clinics to compete.

00:27:59.377 --> 00:28:01.012
The problem is if you
have a monopoly power

00:28:01.012 --> 00:28:02.514 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's just so
dominant in that area.

00:28:02.514 --> 00:28:04.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you're allowed to
tell the health insurance,

00:28:04.315 --> 00:28:06.418 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you work with this guy,
we won't work with you

00:28:06.418 --> 00:28:08.053 align:start size:88% position:13%
and everything's secret,
and you can't say

00:28:08.053 --> 00:28:09.587
what we're charging you.

00:28:09.587 --> 00:28:13.024
They basically become these
things that kill competition.

00:28:13.024 --> 00:28:14.492 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's fundamentally,
un-American.

00:28:14.492 --> 00:28:16.995 align:start position:0%
America works better if you
have competition in these areas.

00:28:16.995 --> 00:28:18.496
And there's lots of ways.

00:28:18.496 --> 00:28:20.665 align:start size:81% position:19%
There's lots of
good academic work

00:28:20.665 --> 00:28:23.368
on measuring what a
monopoly is or not.

00:28:23.368 --> 00:28:25.003 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's comical to me
that we're going after

00:28:25.003 --> 00:28:26.805
the tech monopolies
to start right now,

00:28:26.805 --> 00:28:28.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
when we have a much bigger
problem in this country

00:28:28.573 --> 00:28:31.309
of the healthcare law plays
on all sorts of local levels.

00:28:31.309 --> 00:28:34.446
And if the FTC should use
the academic literature,

00:28:34.446 --> 00:28:37.115
that's been written by both
sides the last 70 years,

00:28:37.115 --> 00:28:40.719 align:start size:84% position:16%
the conservative
view of antitrust, in
my view is correct.

00:28:40.719 --> 00:28:42.420 align:start size:97% position:3%
The Borkean view, basically
that Bork created,

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:43.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
as one of the great
legal scholars,

00:28:43.888 --> 00:28:46.357 align:start position:0%
which is you look at the impact
on consumer and the prices.

00:28:46.357 --> 00:28:48.226
And you can very
clearly see places

00:28:48.226 --> 00:28:49.427
where we're spending two, three,

00:28:49.427 --> 00:28:51.062
four times as much
for certain services.

00:28:51.062 --> 00:28:52.597
It's very clearly a monopoly.

00:28:52.597 --> 00:28:55.667
If there's any view where the
FTC's supposed to do something

00:28:55.667 --> 00:28:56.901
to me, that should be it.

00:28:57.902 --> 00:28:59.104
And again, if you want to say

00:28:59.104 --> 00:29:00.638 align:start size:88% position:13%
that you don't need
government at all, sure.

00:29:00.638 --> 00:29:02.240
If you got rid of all
healthcare regulations,

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:03.708
and made it really
easy to compete,

00:29:03.708 --> 00:29:05.410
maybe this problem
would solve itself,

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:06.978
but we're not gonna get
rid of all those things.

00:29:06.978 --> 00:29:09.013
And so therefore
let's find some way

00:29:09.013 --> 00:29:11.216
to get competition
and make it healthy.

00:29:11.216 --> 00:29:14.586 align:start size:88% position:13%
- So what would the
right system look like?

00:29:14.586 --> 00:29:18.189
Would it be just a lot
of individual companies

00:29:18.189 --> 00:29:20.258 align:start size:94% position:6%
that are sort of corralled
by the government

00:29:20.258 --> 00:29:22.193
to have to play fairly?

00:29:22.193 --> 00:29:24.095
What does it actually look like?

00:29:24.095 --> 00:29:25.630
- You want it to be as
open as possible, right?

00:29:25.630 --> 00:29:27.298
You want to be as
open as possible.

00:29:27.298 --> 00:29:29.134
You want new types
of competition to
be able to come in.

00:29:29.134 --> 00:29:30.935
You want people to be able to
choose their own health plans.

00:29:30.935 --> 00:29:32.537 align:start size:97% position:3%
One of the things government
messed up, as you know,

00:29:32.537 --> 00:29:34.739
is in the 1940s in World War II,

00:29:34.739 --> 00:29:36.641 align:start size:94% position:6%
we made it so you
couldn't pay people more,

00:29:36.641 --> 00:29:38.309 align:start size:81% position:19%
but you could give
them benefits.

00:29:38.309 --> 00:29:41.079 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we made healthcare
a tax-free benefit.

00:29:41.079 --> 00:29:42.614 align:start size:84% position:16%
So it created the
system in our country

00:29:42.614 --> 00:29:44.883 align:start size:88% position:13%
where we started
expecting to get things,

00:29:44.883 --> 00:29:46.317 align:start size:84% position:16%
of course, people want
to pay people more,

00:29:46.317 --> 00:29:47.819 align:start size:97% position:3%
so they just gave them better
and better health plans.

00:29:47.819 --> 00:29:49.621
And so everyone that used
to having a health plan

00:29:49.621 --> 00:29:52.423
from their company tax-free,
and not choosing it themselves.

00:29:52.423 --> 00:29:54.592
And so it got rid of
a lot of choice there.

00:29:54.592 --> 00:29:56.060
And people don't even know.

00:29:56.060 --> 00:29:57.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
Some random HR person
who's probably super woke

00:29:57.629 --> 00:30:00.098 align:start size:97% position:3%
is the one choosing your
healthcare plan for you for.

00:30:00.098 --> 00:30:01.299
For a lot of Americans,

00:30:01.299 --> 00:30:02.934 align:start size:84% position:16%
that's not how
things should work.

00:30:02.934 --> 00:30:05.103
And so what it should be,

00:30:06.771 --> 00:30:09.240 align:start size:97% position:3%
it should be that people
choose their own health plan.

00:30:09.240 --> 00:30:10.842
Avik Roy has something,
Senator Braun, I think,

00:30:10.842 --> 00:30:13.444
put out yesterday, which was a
really great new health plan.

00:30:13.444 --> 00:30:17.348
And what it is is basically,
it's going to cover everyone.

00:30:17.348 --> 00:30:19.417
It's not gonna cover rich
people, it's means tested.

00:30:19.417 --> 00:30:21.619
So anyone 55 or under, you're
not going to get Medicare,

00:30:21.619 --> 00:30:23.087
if you're rich going forward.

00:30:24.455 --> 00:30:25.723 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's gonna cover
everyone using the market,

00:30:25.723 --> 00:30:27.225 align:start size:84% position:16%
which is that everyone
gets to choose

00:30:27.225 --> 00:30:30.395
their own private insurance
company and any new startups,

00:30:30.395 --> 00:30:32.831
the people have to choose the
health insurance themselves.

00:30:32.831 --> 00:30:34.732
And then in the meantime
we're gonna have antitrust,

00:30:34.732 --> 00:30:36.301
we're gonna have transparency,

00:30:36.301 --> 00:30:38.770
and we're gonna push back on
these really sketchy tactics

00:30:38.770 --> 00:30:40.538 line:0%
people use on IP, on drugs,

00:30:40.538 --> 00:30:41.773 line:0%
where they've had
the IP for decades,

00:30:41.773 --> 00:30:43.274 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
and they're still
keeping it expensive.

00:30:43.274 --> 00:30:45.610 line:0%
It's a combination
of using markets,

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:49.614 align:start size:88% position:13%
using private insurance
and means testing.

00:30:49.614 --> 00:30:51.616 align:start size:97% position:3%
This is a compromise again,
and covering everyone.

00:30:51.616 --> 00:30:53.451 align:start size:84% position:16%
But basically
everything's private,

00:30:53.451 --> 00:30:56.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the bottom gets
covered by the government.

00:30:56.087 --> 00:30:58.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that combination
would save money and work.

00:30:58.690 --> 00:31:00.024 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think we need a
compromise like this

00:31:00.024 --> 00:31:01.092
to keep our health system free.

00:31:01.092 --> 00:31:03.228
- Yeah, have you been tracking

00:31:03.228 --> 00:31:05.997
what Jared Kushner
is doing with Oscar?

00:31:05.997 --> 00:31:08.499
Is that related to any of the
types of things you're doing?

00:31:08.499 --> 00:31:10.168
- That's Jared's brother Josh.

00:31:10.168 --> 00:31:11.102 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Sorry. Josh, yeah.
- He's a good friend of mine

00:31:11.102 --> 00:31:14.005
Josh Kushner, Jared is very busy

00:31:14.005 --> 00:31:15.640
making peace in the Middle East.

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:16.908
- (laughing) Jared's got
other things going on.

00:31:16.908 --> 00:31:18.576
Yeah, yeah, yeah,
yeah. Josh, Josh.

00:31:18.576 --> 00:31:21.479
- I am a fan of the
Kushner brothers.

00:31:21.479 --> 00:31:23.014 align:start size:84% position:16%
A lot of my friends
in the Valley say, oh,

00:31:23.014 --> 00:31:24.782
that Jared guy must be horrible.

00:31:24.782 --> 00:31:26.584
I show him some stuff he's
doing or connect them to him.

00:31:26.584 --> 00:31:28.052 align:start size:84% position:16%
And they end up
liking him in person.

00:31:28.052 --> 00:31:30.388
He's actually a pretty
smart guy, in my opinion.

00:31:30.388 --> 00:31:33.258 align:start size:84% position:16%
Josh has done a
great job with Oscar.

00:31:33.258 --> 00:31:35.526 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's a lot of
ways Oscar uses data

00:31:35.526 --> 00:31:38.329
and uses telemedicine,
no one had done before.

00:31:38.329 --> 00:31:39.864
It was fascinating.

00:31:39.864 --> 00:31:41.299
We had a bunch of clinics
on the platform of course.

00:31:41.299 --> 00:31:44.435 align:start position:0%
This is a health insurance
company that's growing quickly.

00:31:44.435 --> 00:31:46.070
I've put a $100 million into it,

00:31:46.070 --> 00:31:48.573
it's one of my 20 or
30 big investments.

00:31:48.573 --> 00:31:50.375 align:start size:88% position:13%
And there was like this
pediatrician group.

00:31:50.375 --> 00:31:52.210 align:start size:97% position:3%
And they said, how come
we're not on your platform?

00:31:52.210 --> 00:31:53.478
And we told him, well, listen,

00:31:53.478 --> 00:31:55.079
you guys spend three
times as much on tests

00:31:55.079 --> 00:31:57.282 align:start size:97% position:3%
as any of the other
pediatricians in this area.

00:31:57.282 --> 00:31:58.516
And they said, oh, wow.
We didn't know that.

00:31:58.516 --> 00:32:00.118 align:start size:84% position:16%
No one had ever
told us that before.

00:32:00.118 --> 00:32:02.487 align:start size:84% position:16%
And we said, wow,
what a strange space.

00:32:02.487 --> 00:32:04.355
These people have no idea how
much money they're spending.

00:32:04.355 --> 00:32:06.291
It's such a broken area

00:32:06.291 --> 00:32:07.191 align:start size:84% position:16%
that none of the other
insurance companies

00:32:07.191 --> 00:32:08.426
had even told them that,

00:32:08.426 --> 00:32:10.361
that they were doing
things unnecessarily.

00:32:11.796 --> 00:32:13.998
Oscar's done a lot to push
health insurance to use data

00:32:13.998 --> 00:32:17.702
and to be a better force,
so I'm very bullish on it.

00:32:17.702 --> 00:32:18.970
- Yeah.

00:32:18.970 --> 00:32:21.806
I know one of your other
main places of interest

00:32:21.806 --> 00:32:23.241
is criminal justice reform.

00:32:23.241 --> 00:32:26.044 align:start size:97% position:3%
I feel like this is one
where almost everyone agrees,

00:32:26.044 --> 00:32:29.514
something should be done
and there can be something

00:32:29.514 --> 00:32:30.982
that's bipartisan.

00:32:30.982 --> 00:32:33.217 align:start size:97% position:3%
And in many ways, Trump seems
to be trying to do things

00:32:33.217 --> 00:32:36.487 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I thought lefties were
fighting for, for a long time.

00:32:36.487 --> 00:32:38.790 align:start size:84% position:16%
How can we fix this
clearly broken system?

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:41.159
- Trump and Jared have
done a good job on this.

00:32:41.159 --> 00:32:42.427
There's a lot of
people on the right

00:32:42.427 --> 00:32:44.362
who are suspicious
of some of this.

00:32:44.362 --> 00:32:47.832
I don't blame them, a lot
of criminal justice reform

00:32:47.832 --> 00:32:49.867
is being pitched, like
getting rid of the bail,

00:32:49.867 --> 00:32:52.070 align:start size:97% position:3%
or, you make it so you arrest
these guys, they get out

00:32:52.070 --> 00:32:53.471 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you have to arrest them
again and then get out.

00:32:53.471 --> 00:32:54.973
You have to arrest them again.

00:32:54.973 --> 00:32:56.174
There's a lot of ridiculous
criminal justice reform.

00:32:56.174 --> 00:32:57.709
I'm not for any of that.

00:32:57.709 --> 00:32:59.877
The part that I
am for, is again,

00:32:59.877 --> 00:33:01.746
how can you fix the systems
that are failing people?

00:33:01.746 --> 00:33:03.948 align:start size:84% position:16%
So, a few systems that
are failing people,

00:33:03.948 --> 00:33:06.084 align:start size:84% position:16%
one of them is
probation and parole.

00:33:06.084 --> 00:33:08.686
There's literally amazing
probation, parole officers

00:33:08.686 --> 00:33:11.322
who really spend time
deploying the tactics

00:33:11.322 --> 00:33:13.524
to lower recidivism,
to help these people.

00:33:13.524 --> 00:33:15.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
They see their job as
they're serving these people

00:33:15.159 --> 00:33:16.594
and they want to keep
them out of prison

00:33:16.594 --> 00:33:18.196
and they want to help
them succeed in life.

00:33:18.196 --> 00:33:19.897
And they want to
be easy on them,

00:33:19.897 --> 00:33:21.065
easy on when they
can't hard on them

00:33:21.065 --> 00:33:22.467
when it's helpful for them.

00:33:22.467 --> 00:33:24.202 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there's other
probation, parole officers

00:33:24.202 --> 00:33:25.436
who are absolutely horrible,

00:33:25.436 --> 00:33:27.005
and they're nasty
to these people

00:33:27.005 --> 00:33:29.240
and they get a job
but it's too far away

00:33:29.240 --> 00:33:30.575
and they don't have time
to see the parole officers.

00:33:30.575 --> 00:33:32.310
They throw him back in prison,

00:33:32.310 --> 00:33:34.078 align:start size:81% position:19%
Whatever it is,
they're just like,

00:33:34.078 --> 00:33:35.546
make things so tough on them.

00:33:35.546 --> 00:33:37.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so the question is,
how can you have incentives

00:33:37.448 --> 00:33:39.350
from probation or parole?

00:33:39.350 --> 00:33:40.952
I'm not usually a
fan of California.

00:33:40.952 --> 00:33:42.520 align:start size:84% position:16%
We were talking
about this earlier.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:44.055 align:start size:97% position:3%
I know you're still
staying there in the state.

00:33:44.055 --> 00:33:46.290
But one thing they did
really well, 11 years ago

00:33:46.290 --> 00:33:49.594
is a bipartisan bill where
they changed the incentives

00:33:49.594 --> 00:33:51.329
for county probation offices.

00:33:51.329 --> 00:33:53.097
They actually get to
keep some of the money

00:33:53.097 --> 00:33:54.932 align:start size:97% position:3%
if they didn't send as
many people back to prison,

00:33:54.932 --> 00:33:56.601 align:start size:84% position:16%
as long as the crime
rate didn't go up

00:33:56.601 --> 00:33:58.169
and it worked extremely well,

00:33:58.169 --> 00:34:00.471 align:start position:0%
saved the state a billion
dollars over the last 11 years.

00:34:00.471 --> 00:34:02.473
And Montana, did
something similar.

00:34:02.473 --> 00:34:04.642 align:start position:0%
So we're taking those successes
and we're teaching people,

00:34:04.642 --> 00:34:06.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
okay, let's do this for
California parole now too.

00:34:06.444 --> 00:34:08.146 align:start size:97% position:3%
Let's actually have incentives
for the parole officers

00:34:08.146 --> 00:34:09.647
actually care about
doing their jobs,

00:34:09.647 --> 00:34:11.282
because what happened as
soon as you pass that law,

00:34:11.282 --> 00:34:13.651
is all these offices started
studying and sharing notes,

00:34:13.651 --> 00:34:14.919
what works to low recidivism?

00:34:14.919 --> 00:34:17.055
How do we do this? How
do we care about this?

00:34:17.055 --> 00:34:18.556
Suddenly everyone's
getting measured.

00:34:18.556 --> 00:34:20.792 align:start size:88% position:13%
Bob you've sent
everyone back to prison

00:34:20.792 --> 00:34:22.894
the last seven years, that's
really hurting our numbers.

00:34:22.894 --> 00:34:25.596
Maybe you should care about,
not just sending him back.

00:34:25.596 --> 00:34:26.697
Maybe you should try,
actually work with them

00:34:26.697 --> 00:34:27.532
and try to help them.

00:34:27.532 --> 00:34:28.733
And here's some tactics

00:34:28.733 --> 00:34:30.201 align:start size:84% position:16%
and techniques we
found work, you know?

00:34:30.201 --> 00:34:32.103 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so it's amazing having
accountability in these areas,

00:34:32.103 --> 00:34:35.339 align:start size:97% position:3%
what that could do.
- Yeah. What about education?

00:34:35.339 --> 00:34:37.041
What's going on here?

00:34:37.041 --> 00:34:39.710 align:start position:0%
It seems to me, choice,
choice, choice, choice, choice.

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:42.747
And yet that seems to be
a scary thing to people.

00:34:42.747 --> 00:34:44.549
- Yeah. Well, I'm generally
obviously a fan of choice.

00:34:44.549 --> 00:34:46.384 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's the thing we're
talking about in every area

00:34:46.384 --> 00:34:47.618
is competition and choice.

00:34:47.618 --> 00:34:50.021
But there's different
levels of education.

00:34:51.189 --> 00:34:52.423
All of them are
really important.

00:34:52.423 --> 00:34:53.791
We've gotta get these
kids when they're young

00:34:53.791 --> 00:34:55.026 align:start size:84% position:16%
and make sure they're
not programmed

00:34:55.026 --> 00:34:56.994
by Marxists as little
kids, that's important.

00:34:56.994 --> 00:34:59.363 align:start size:97% position:3%
But in terms of the
incentives, we can work on.

00:34:59.363 --> 00:35:01.933
Incentives are easier to do at
the latter part of education,

00:35:01.933 --> 00:35:02.967 align:start size:84% position:16%
because you can
measure the results.

00:35:02.967 --> 00:35:04.602
And one of my favorite programs

00:35:04.602 --> 00:35:05.870
to really go after right now,

00:35:05.870 --> 00:35:07.972
there's something
called Pell Grants.

00:35:07.972 --> 00:35:09.440 align:start size:88% position:13%
The Pell Grants were
created for good reason.

00:35:09.440 --> 00:35:11.142
It's really, well-meaning,
they're trying to help people

00:35:11.142 --> 00:35:13.211 align:start size:84% position:16%
from bad socioeconomic
backgrounds

00:35:13.211 --> 00:35:15.213 align:start size:97% position:3%
or lower socioeconomic
backgrounds succeed in life

00:35:15.213 --> 00:35:16.681
and send them to college.

00:35:16.681 --> 00:35:18.382 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the problem is, is there's
no accountability at all.

00:35:18.382 --> 00:35:20.451
So we spend $30 billion a year

00:35:20.451 --> 00:35:21.719
on Pell Grants in this country,

00:35:21.719 --> 00:35:24.021 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the results are just
completely different.

00:35:24.021 --> 00:35:26.858
There's some schools
that do a great job.

00:35:26.858 --> 00:35:28.459
And for the exact
same type of kids,

00:35:28.459 --> 00:35:29.794
they'll have kept
twice the salary

00:35:29.794 --> 00:35:32.396
coming out of those
schools as others.

00:35:32.396 --> 00:35:34.198
When you think about university

00:35:34.198 --> 00:35:35.833 align:start size:84% position:16%
is not just about
getting a high salary,

00:35:35.833 --> 00:35:37.068 align:start size:81% position:19%
I think we can
all agree on that.

00:35:37.068 --> 00:35:40.838 align:start size:84% position:16%
University is about
being a great citizen,

00:35:40.838 --> 00:35:42.507
to live in a free republic.

00:35:42.507 --> 00:35:44.108
It is about moral action.

00:35:44.108 --> 00:35:46.644 align:start position:0%
Those are both legit things
for universities to care about.

00:35:46.644 --> 00:35:48.379 align:start size:84% position:16%
But the third thing
definitely is,

00:35:48.379 --> 00:35:50.515
learning how to get
skills, to get a job,

00:35:50.515 --> 00:35:52.316 align:start size:88% position:13%
learning how to actually
succeed in life.

00:35:52.316 --> 00:35:54.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
And if we're going to
spend $30 billion a year,

00:35:54.585 --> 00:35:55.953
maybe part of the
Pell Grant program

00:35:55.953 --> 00:35:57.922 align:start size:88% position:13%
should care that they're
getting skills or not

00:35:57.922 --> 00:35:59.423
when they're going
to these schools.

00:35:59.423 --> 00:36:01.259
And because the results
are literally two to one,

00:36:01.259 --> 00:36:03.895 align:start size:97% position:3%
the question's, okay, these
really, really crappy schools,

00:36:03.895 --> 00:36:05.563 align:start size:84% position:16%
where they probably
all become protestors,

00:36:05.563 --> 00:36:07.165 align:start size:94% position:6%
or God knows what they're
gonna do afterwards,

00:36:07.165 --> 00:36:08.633 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause they're going
to have any jobs.

00:36:08.633 --> 00:36:10.935 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the average salary is
21,000 or whatever from them,

00:36:10.935 --> 00:36:12.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe we shouldn't be
giving as many Pell grants

00:36:12.670 --> 00:36:13.871
to go to those schools.

00:36:13.871 --> 00:36:15.006
And these great ones
where people are making

00:36:15.006 --> 00:36:16.440
more than twice as much as that

00:36:16.440 --> 00:36:18.009 align:start size:94% position:6%
maybe they should be
getting more Pell Grants.

00:36:18.009 --> 00:36:19.510
The question is how
do you put incentives

00:36:19.510 --> 00:36:21.846
into these programs to take
into account of the data?

00:36:21.846 --> 00:36:23.080 align:start size:81% position:19%
Right now we don't
do that at all.

00:36:23.080 --> 00:36:24.615
And our government
probably should.

00:36:24.615 --> 00:36:26.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah. Do you think
we could just let

00:36:26.484 --> 00:36:29.387
